 \paragraph{STEP 1}
The hyperbola is centered at the point $(h,k).$  Note that the transverse axis of the hyperbola is vertical.  Therefore, $h$ is the same as the x-coordinate of the point of  intersection of the hyperbola and the horizontal line that passes through the center of  the hyperbola.  It is also the x-coordinate of the point where the hyperbola intersects the dashed  vertical line to the right of center.
First, find $h.$
Because the full equation of the hyperbola is \[\frac{(y - k)^2}{a^2} - \frac{(x - h)^2}{b^2} = 1,\] every value  of x that makes the first term zero makes the line \[\frac{(y - k)^2}{a^2} = \frac{(x - h)^2}{b^2}\]  
when $y=k$ and will therefore lie on the reflected hyperbola above the original hyperbola, pointing to where the left  and right branches of the hyperbola both intersect their vertical dashed lines.  Also when $x = h$ the first term becomes 0, so the point on the hyperbola which  $x=h$ refer to, should be in the form  $ yi $. $ k $ is a value that determines where the hyperbola intersects with the horizontal dashed line that passes through its center.
Looking at the move drawn into the problem, it seems that
- The real parts of every point of the left vertical  branch of the of the hyperbola should have a real part greater than $h.$
- The real parts of the points on the dashed line make the first line  zero. Any dashed vertical line to the right of the center have the real  parts that are to the right of $h$.
## Step 2
The first of the horizontal lines that intersects a dashed vertical line to the right of center intersects the dashed line where the left and right horizontal dashed lines have been deleted in the problem.
Becuase of the \textit{why} above stated for the first term of the equation of the hyperbola, we can conclude the set \begin{align*} \{ y \quad | \quad y = k \} \end{align*} is a \textit{horizontal} line.
The line  \begin{align*} h + 10 \end{align*} is the only horizontal line that intersects $\textss links$ new dashed line. Then it must intersect this line where the vertical and the  horizontal lines making up the point $(h, k)$. Hence, \begin{align*} k = h + 5 \end{align*}
## Step 3
Now we turn our attention to the distance between $(0, 5)$ and $(10, 5)$, which is $10$.
We will use that later to find $a$, as a distance along the hyperbola that moves in a $90^\circ$ angle from the center of the graph. Therefore, that distance is $2a$.
This horizontal distance is equal to the horizontal distance $\textों$$ tunedt $(10, -5)$, so we can conclude that the distance from $(0, 0)$ to $(10, -5)$ is $5\sqrt{2}+10$.
Thus, we can find that $a$ by dividing that value of the latter distance by 2.
\begin{align*}
a & = \frac{ 5 \sqrt 2 + 10}{2}
\end{align*}
## Step 4 
We now use $a$ to find $b$. A value on the vertical dashed line that passes through center will have the vertical distance $b$ to a value on the left branch that  satisfies that the distance from center to the point on the vertical dashed line is congruent with 4 the distance from center  to a point from the right side where this  condition is true. We already k= h + 5, so we have the point $(h + 4,5)$ representing the   location of the former, also $(h+10, -5)$ to represent the latter.
Taking into consideration both of those measurements, we have
\begin{align*}
b & = \frac{  \sqrt{(a-5)^2 + a ^2} } {2}
\end{align*}

## Step 5:
Here we find $h + k + a + b. \begin{align*}
h+k+a+b & = h+(h+5)+\dfrac{5 \sqrt 2 + 10}{2} + \dfrac{ \sqrt{(5\sqrt 2 +10 - 5 )^2  + (\dfrac{5 \sqrt 2 +10}{2})^2}}{2}}
\end{align*}

## Step 6
Simplifying the equation using algebra.
\begin{align*}
2(h+k+a+b)& = 2h + 2h + 10 + 5\sqrt 2 + 10 + \sqrt{ (5\sqrt 2 + 5)^2  + \left(\dfrac{5 \sqrt 2 +10}{2}\right)^2}  \\
10 + 10\sqrt 2 + \sqrt{  (5\sqrt 2 + 5)^2  + \left(\dfrac{5 \sqrt 2 +10}{2}\right)^2}
\end{align*}
