\begin{solution}
Let $b$ represent the number of ways Mr. Brennan can pick 3 boys, and let $g$ represent the number of ways he can pick 2 girls.  By the addition principle for permutations of sets, we need to calculate how many ways he can pick 3 boys and 2 girls separately, and then add the number of ways he can pick the group.

The first step is to choose the 3 boys.  Mr. Brennan has 7 boys to choose from.  We can represent the boys that will be a part of the presentation with the symbols $\bullet$ and $\circ$.  Since Mr. Brennan just needs to pick 3, and the order of his picks does not matter, we are calculating the combination of 7 boys taken 3 at a time, denoted $\binom{7}{3}$:
\\image[
\begin{tabular}{ccccccl}
& & $B_4$ & & & & \\
$\bullet \quad \bullet \quad \bullet$ & & & & & 
\end{tabular}
]
We can find the number of ways to choose 3 boys, $b$, using the formula for combinations:
b = \frac{n \times (n - 1) \times (n -2)}{3 \times 2 \times 1}
\\= \frac{7 \times 6 \times 5}{3 \times 2 \times 1}
\\= \frac{210}{6}
\\= 35.

Next, we need to find the number of ways that Mr. Brennan can pick 2 girls out of 4.  We also represent the girls with $\bullet$ and $\circ$, shown below so that $\circ$ represents 3 girls and $\bullet$ represents the 1 girl he does not choose:
\\image[
\begin{tabular}{ccccccl}
& & $G_1$ & & & & \\
\bullet \quad \bullet & & & & &
\end{tabular}
]
The number of ways to choose $g$ 2 girls, choose 2 girls out of 4, use the combination formula
g=\frac{n \times (n-1)}{2 \times 1} \\
\)= \frac{4 \times 3}{2 \times 1}
\\= \frac{12}{2}
\\= 6
